      "text": "To find the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of one foot, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches.\n2. Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot.\n3. Compute the ratio of the two volumes.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches.\nThe volume \\( V \\) of a cube is given by the formula:\n\\[\nV = s^3\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is the edge length of the cube. For a cube with an edge length of six inches:\n\\[\nV_1 = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot.\nFirst, we need to convert the edge length from feet to inches. Since there are 12 inches in a foot:\n\\[\n1 \\text{ foot} = 12 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nFor a cube with an edge length of one foot:\n\\[\nV_2 = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Compute the ratio of the two volumes.\nThe ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of one foot is:\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_1}{V_2} = \\frac{216}{1728}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 216 and 1728. The GCD of 216 and 1728 is 216.\n\nDividing both the numerator and the denominator by 216:\n\\[\n\\frac{216}{1728} = \\frac{216 \\div 216}{1728 \\div 216} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of six inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of one foot is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\n\\]",
